Make the simulated gimbal report its attitude the way a real one does.

PX4's simulated gimbal builds its attitude from the gimbal IMU in Gazebo:

    src/modules/simulation/gz_bridge/GZGimbal.cpp, gimbalIMUCallback()
        _q_gimbal = q_ENU_to_NED * q_gimbal_FLU * q_FLU_to_FRD.inversed();

A Gazebo IMU reports orientation against the world, so that quaternion holds the
camera attitude in the earth frame. publishDeviceAttitude() then sends it with
one flag and nothing else:

        gimbal_att.device_flags = DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_IN_VEHICLE_FRAME;

Two things are wrong there. The label says vehicle frame while the data is earth
frame. Worse, the report carries no ROLL_LOCK, PITCH_LOCK or YAW_LOCK bit, and
those bits are how the MAVLink specification tells a consumer which frame the
quaternion is in:

    GIMBAL_DEVICE_ATTITUDE_STATUS, mavlink common.xml
      If the flag GIMBAL_DEVICE_FLAGS_YAW_LOCK is set, then they are relative to
      absolute North (earth frame), else they are relative to the vehicle
      heading (vehicle frame).

A real gimbal sends the lock bits of the mode it is in. PX4 commands ROLL_LOCK
and PITCH_LOCK by default (src/modules/gimbal/output.h, _absolute_angle), the
Chimera flight code commands the same 12, and the device reports 12 back. The
consumer builds the reference frame from those bits, then applies the quaternion
to that frame.

This patch makes GZGimbal do the three things a real device does.

1. Report the lock bits of the last setpoint. GZGimbal already keeps them in
   _gimbal_device_flags and did nothing with them.
2. Set YAW_IN_EARTH_FRAME with YAW_LOCK, and YAW_IN_VEHICLE_FRAME without it.
   The specification tells a new implementation to set one of the two.
3. Move the quaternion into the frame those flags declare, with the airframe
   attitude from vehicle_attitude:

       YAW_LOCK                       earth frame    report q as it is
       ROLL_LOCK or PITCH_LOCK only   vehicle frame  divide the heading off
       no lock bit                    airframe       divide the attitude off

   The division is on the LEFT. An earth referenced attitude is the airframe
   attitude followed by the rotation of the gimbal, q_abs = q_vehicle * q_rel,
   so q_rel = conj(q_vehicle) * q_abs.

The patch also fills delta_yaw, the yaw between the earth frame and the vehicle
frame. That value lets a consumer move the report from one frame to the other.

WHAT THIS DOES NOT DO.

The joints still ride on the airframe. GZGimbal writes the setpoint angles onto
Gazebo joint controllers, so the simulated gimbal holds nothing against the
horizon, whatever the flags say. The lock bits report the mode that was
commanded, as on a real device. The quaternion reports where the camera is, and
that comes from the IMU, so a frame built from this report is correct even while
the gimbal fails to hold the horizon. docs/px4-simulated-gimbal.md has the rest.

The consumer in this stack is MAVInsight. It builds d<N>_gimbal_frame_ref from
the lock bits in models/vehicle.py, then applies the quaternion to that frame in
models/sensor.py. With the flags above, the camera frame it builds is exact.
MAVInsight needed one fix of its own for the YAW_LOCK case: its earth reference
frame was aligned with ENU, and the MAVLink earth frame is NED, so the camera
frame came out 90 degrees off. That is a fault against real hardware too.

Read from the code and proved with arithmetic, not measured in flight. Check
d<N>_gimbal_frame against the vehicle heading before you trust it.

    cd src/PX4-Autopilot
    git apply ../../patches/px4-gzgimbal-frame.patch

